WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:03.070
- As I mentioned, we have a
very special guest this hour.

00:00:03.070 --> 00:00:04.960
She never does interviews. Like, never.

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:06.670
So I'm feeling super special right now.

00:00:06.670 --> 00:00:10.340
She's a fellow Texan, a teacher,
a mother, a loving wife,

00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:11.270
and the creator of

00:00:11.270 --> 00:00:13.960
the Laura Bush Foundation
for America's Libraries,

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:15.460
which supports access to books

00:00:15.460 --> 00:00:17.270
for all kids across the nation.

00:00:17.270 --> 00:00:19.990
Please welcome former First
Lady of the United States,

00:00:19.990 --> 00:00:21.187
Laura Bush.

00:00:21.187 --> 00:00:24.020
(audience cheers)

00:00:25.804 --> 00:00:26.637
Hello!

00:00:27.890 --> 00:00:28.723
- Hey, Kelly.

00:00:28.723 --> 00:00:30.523
- Hello, it's so nice to see you.

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:33.290
- Thanks. Thanks for having me on.

00:00:33.290 --> 00:00:34.900
- Oh my gosh, thanks for joining us.

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:36.530
I'm very, we're honored to have you,

00:00:36.530 --> 00:00:38.400
and obviously we're very honored

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:40.500
because we're going to be
honoring teachers today.

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:43.810
So, who was the first teacher
who had an impact on you?

00:00:43.810 --> 00:00:46.480
- Well, I had a second grade
teacher that I really loved.

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:48.210
I wanted to be just like her.

00:00:48.210 --> 00:00:50.760
Her name was Miss Nagy, Charlene Nagy,

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:54.780
and I stayed her friend for
the whole rest of her life.

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:57.210
I got to invite her to the White House.

00:00:57.210 --> 00:01:00.690
There's, there you see her, right there.

00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:02.570
And she was really the influence on me

00:01:02.570 --> 00:01:05.110
that made me want to teach second grade.

00:01:05.110 --> 00:01:07.400
- Wow. I bet, then she did have an impact.

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:09.780
What was your first teaching job?

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:11.550
Cause my, I remember
when, my mom's a teacher,

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:13.392
and I remember even when she
started student teaching,

00:01:13.392 --> 00:01:14.740
those were hard days,

00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:17.430
especially middle school was really rough,

00:01:17.430 --> 00:01:19.620
the kids in puberty, all of that,

00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:22.290
but were you nervous or was it hard?

00:01:22.290 --> 00:01:23.200
- Sure, I was nervous.

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:26.207
I taught my very first
year I taught in Dallas

00:01:26.207 --> 00:01:28.820
at Longfellow Elementary,

00:01:28.820 --> 00:01:30.790
which happened to be
the school on my street.

00:01:30.790 --> 00:01:34.470
I could walk down to the
school that was there and, yes,

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:36.340
I was very nervous.

00:01:36.340 --> 00:01:38.410
And then after that year, I thought,

00:01:38.410 --> 00:01:40.640
I think I need to go work downtown.

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:42.320
I'd moved to Houston the next year.

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:45.330
And I got a job downtown
in a brokerage firm

00:01:45.330 --> 00:01:49.310
and found out that nothing is
as entertaining as teaching

00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:52.420
because with all those little
personalities in the room,

00:01:52.420 --> 00:01:53.810
there's never a dull moment.

00:01:53.810 --> 00:01:57.430
There might be some bad moments
that you'd like to skip,

00:01:57.430 --> 00:02:00.760
but in general it's really very, very fun.

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:03.090
- But what made you want
to move from teaching

00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:04.130
to being a librarian?

00:02:04.130 --> 00:02:05.080
I love books.

00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:07.150
Is it just your love of reading?

00:02:07.150 --> 00:02:07.983
- That was it.

00:02:07.983 --> 00:02:09.840
I mean, that was my
favorite part of teaching.

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:12.806
And that was reading
stories to kids, working,

00:02:12.806 --> 00:02:15.210
talking about literature,
children's literature.

00:02:15.210 --> 00:02:17.420
And so I went back to graduate school

00:02:17.420 --> 00:02:19.060
and got a degree in library science

00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:21.950
and worked at a public library in Houston

00:02:21.950 --> 00:02:24.120
and then worked in school libraries.

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:28.120
That's what I was doing in
Austin when George and I married.

00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:30.610
- Oh my gosh, I loved our public library.

00:02:30.610 --> 00:02:32.830
I support them wholeheartedly right now.

00:02:32.830 --> 00:02:34.160
It's so hard cause they're struggling,

00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:35.040
a lot of the public libraries,

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:38.160
but that is where I got,
because we were so poor, there,

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:39.830
we didn't get, we didn't
have money to buy books,

00:02:39.830 --> 00:02:40.663
so I'd get all my books.

00:02:40.663 --> 00:02:41.760
They'd have, like, tapes.

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:42.820
So I'd get all my music.

00:02:42.820 --> 00:02:44.820
They had like a catalog section for that.

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:48.040
I loved our public library,
and I just love reading.

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:50.850
My mom really invested
that in me at a young age.

00:02:50.850 --> 00:02:52.640
But what were some of the first books

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:54.750
that you remember loving as a kid?

00:02:54.750 --> 00:02:56.190
- Well, of course I loved all the

00:02:56.190 --> 00:02:58.157
Laura Ingalls Wilder books,

00:02:58.157 --> 00:02:59.450
"The Little House on the Prairie,"

00:02:59.450 --> 00:03:01.490
and "The Little House in the Big Woods,"

00:03:01.490 --> 00:03:05.280
all those Laura, you know,
she was, had my same name

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:06.749
and she had brown hair.

00:03:06.749 --> 00:03:10.780
And so you get to, I got to
sort of grow up with Laura.

00:03:10.780 --> 00:03:11.763
- That's amazing.

00:03:11.763 --> 00:03:13.580
My, the book that changed me

00:03:13.580 --> 00:03:15.500
cause my mom would always
try and get me to read,

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:17.470
and until a certain age in middle school,

00:03:17.470 --> 00:03:19.590
I was kind of like, I don't want to read.

00:03:19.590 --> 00:03:21.990
And then I read "Matilda,"

00:03:21.990 --> 00:03:24.810
and I thought it was the
most magical thing ever.

00:03:24.810 --> 00:03:27.827
And I always say, from
that book, I kind of,

00:03:27.827 --> 00:03:31.170
I figured out your
imagination has no budget,

00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:32.860
but movie sets do.

00:03:32.860 --> 00:03:35.210
And so you can create
the most magical places,

00:03:35.210 --> 00:03:36.320
like with books.

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:37.480
And I fell in love with that,

00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:39.260
and "The Box Car Children"
was my thing as well.

00:03:39.260 --> 00:03:40.820
But, you've just read -

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:41.780
- I remember "Boxcar."

00:03:41.780 --> 00:03:44.430
- Yeah? Oh, I love those.

00:03:44.430 --> 00:03:47.280
But you've just released a
summer reading list for kids.

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:49.290
So what are a few of the books on it?

00:03:49.290 --> 00:03:51.860
- Well, of course we had to
put on a Beverly Cleary book.

00:03:51.860 --> 00:03:53.930
We just lost Beverly Cleary,

00:03:53.930 --> 00:03:56.680
and we put on "The Mouse
and the Motorcycle."

00:03:56.680 --> 00:04:00.100
But I remember when Barbara
and Jenna were little,

00:04:00.100 --> 00:04:02.780
Barbara would, wanted to
learn to read so badly,

00:04:02.780 --> 00:04:05.660
and she would read Beverly
Cleary's book "Ribsy,"

00:04:05.660 --> 00:04:07.460
about the dog.

00:04:07.460 --> 00:04:09.780
So we have Beverly Cleary
is one of the books

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:11.590
on that book list.

00:04:11.590 --> 00:04:13.730
- But speaking of books, I said

00:04:13.730 --> 00:04:15.650
this was our biggest giveaway show ever.

00:04:15.650 --> 00:04:16.823
So let's get to it.

00:04:17.731 --> 00:04:18.564
- Great.

00:04:18.564 --> 00:04:20.690
- As Mrs. Bush knows, a
school is built on books.

00:04:20.690 --> 00:04:23.010
And so does Scholastic, which, oh my gosh,

00:04:23.010 --> 00:04:25.240
I loved this little magazine so much.

00:04:25.240 --> 00:04:27.270
It was like the best time of
the year when I was a kid.

00:04:27.270 --> 00:04:29.420
And it was the one-time
my mom would let me shop,

00:04:29.420 --> 00:04:30.470
because we were poor, but she was like,

00:04:30.470 --> 00:04:31.340
I'll invest in the book.

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:33.810
So anyway, for a hundred years,

00:04:33.810 --> 00:04:36.640
Scholastic's mission has
been to serve teachers.

00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:39.880
So to help teachers transition
back into the classroom

00:04:39.880 --> 00:04:41.175
and build their classroom libraries,

00:04:41.175 --> 00:04:44.610
Scholastic is donating
$5,000 worth of books

00:04:44.610 --> 00:04:46.990
to every teacher in our house seats.

00:04:46.990 --> 00:04:49.080
What? That's 500 books each.

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:50.723
- [Laura] Yay, that's great.

00:04:52.138 --> 00:04:52.971
(audience applauds)

00:04:52.971 --> 00:04:55.790
- Oh my gosh, I can't
tell you how happy I was

00:04:55.790 --> 00:04:57.830
every time my mom brought
those Scholastic things home.

00:04:57.830 --> 00:04:59.320
I was like, which ones can I get?

00:04:59.320 --> 00:05:00.820
I was like, I loved that little magazine.

00:05:00.820 --> 00:05:03.170
So, Mrs. Bush, I have to ask you, like,

00:05:03.170 --> 00:05:04.710
how did your love of reading and books

00:05:04.710 --> 00:05:06.740
influence your work as a First Lady?

00:05:06.740 --> 00:05:09.860
- Well, I did, I started a
foundation that gives books

00:05:09.860 --> 00:05:11.490
to school libraries.

00:05:11.490 --> 00:05:14.810
Because, of course, I'd visit
schools all over the country

00:05:14.810 --> 00:05:18.430
and many schools have, and of course,

00:05:18.430 --> 00:05:22.040
usually in the parts of town
where kids need books the most

00:05:22.040 --> 00:05:24.900
need to have books in a library,

00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:29.360
would have so few books,
very few reference materials.

00:05:29.360 --> 00:05:32.590
And so I started a
foundation that gives books

00:05:32.590 --> 00:05:35.960
to, or money for books,
to school libraries.

00:05:35.960 --> 00:05:38.020
So school libraries can
build their collections

00:05:38.020 --> 00:05:39.710
for their kids.

00:05:39.710 --> 00:05:40.543
- [Kelly] That's -

00:05:40.543 --> 00:05:41.900
- [Laura] The Laura Bush Foundation.

00:05:41.900 --> 00:05:44.140
- Absolutely, I love how
your foundation provides

00:05:44.140 --> 00:05:46.670
such opportunity for kids
that would never have that.

00:05:46.670 --> 00:05:48.720
But since your
mother-in-law, Barbara Bush,

00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:51.795
also had that job, you had an advantage

00:05:51.795 --> 00:05:54.260
no other First Lady ever had.

00:05:54.260 --> 00:05:57.120
So did she ever give you any advice?

00:05:57.120 --> 00:05:59.110
- Well, I did have a huge advantage.

00:05:59.110 --> 00:06:01.000
I mean, you can imagine
having Barbara Bush

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:03.160
for a mother-in-law, and I do remember

00:06:03.160 --> 00:06:05.440
when she talked about
what she wanted to do,

00:06:05.440 --> 00:06:07.710
which was also literacy.

00:06:07.710 --> 00:06:10.110
She had started the foundation,

00:06:10.110 --> 00:06:12.680
the Barbara Bush Foundation
for Family Literacy

00:06:12.680 --> 00:06:17.530
that gives, supports
family literacy providers.

00:06:17.530 --> 00:06:19.760
And I think that was
terrific, but she did,

00:06:19.760 --> 00:06:22.751
she really knew that
daughter-in-laws didn't want advice

00:06:22.751 --> 00:06:24.573
from their mother-in-laws.

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:27.727
But one time she told me, she said,

00:06:27.727 --> 00:06:31.420
"Listen. Don't criticize
George's speeches."

00:06:31.420 --> 00:06:34.700
She said that she'd criticized
her George's speech,

00:06:34.700 --> 00:06:37.570
and he'd come home from,
for weeks afterwards

00:06:37.570 --> 00:06:40.780
with letters saying it was the
best speech he'd ever given.

00:06:40.780 --> 00:06:42.210
So, I took her advice.

00:06:42.210 --> 00:06:44.060
This is when we lived in Midland, Texas,

00:06:44.060 --> 00:06:46.320
and George was running for Congress,

00:06:46.320 --> 00:06:48.180
and, which he didn't win.

00:06:48.180 --> 00:06:49.340
And I took her advice.

00:06:49.340 --> 00:06:52.250
So we were driving in, down
from Lubbock to Midland,

00:06:52.250 --> 00:06:54.120
driving, turning into our driveway.

00:06:54.120 --> 00:06:56.590
And George said, "How was my speech?"

00:06:56.590 --> 00:06:59.530
And I said, "Well, it wasn't that good."

00:06:59.530 --> 00:07:01.453
And he drove into the garage wall.

00:07:03.990 --> 00:07:05.780
- You know what, though?
We need those people.

00:07:05.780 --> 00:07:08.570
You need the, we don't want
yes people around us, right?

00:07:08.570 --> 00:07:10.805
You want people that are
going to keep it real.

00:07:10.805 --> 00:07:15.805
- Well I tried not to criticize
him after that in speeches.

00:07:15.940 --> 00:07:18.370
- Well, Mrs. Bush, we asked Dr. Jill Biden

00:07:18.370 --> 00:07:20.680
and Michelle Obama the first thing

00:07:20.680 --> 00:07:22.570
they want to do post pandemic.

00:07:22.570 --> 00:07:25.530
Dr. Biden wanted to go out
for a martini and fries,

00:07:25.530 --> 00:07:26.870
which I loved.

00:07:26.870 --> 00:07:30.510
So what have you checked off
your post pandemic to-do list?

00:07:30.510 --> 00:07:32.610
- Well, hugged my grandchildren.

00:07:32.610 --> 00:07:33.443
- [Kelly] Oh yeah.

00:07:33.443 --> 00:07:36.420
- When George and I were in
New York a couple of weeks ago,

00:07:36.420 --> 00:07:39.160
we got to go by and see all three of them,

00:07:39.160 --> 00:07:42.460
Mila and Poppy and little baby Hal.

00:07:42.460 --> 00:07:44.970
And they were so wild
jumping off the couches

00:07:44.970 --> 00:07:47.150
and acting so wild with us that we decided

00:07:47.150 --> 00:07:49.643
maybe we were too old to be grandparents.

00:07:51.576 --> 00:07:53.050
- That's amazing.

00:07:53.050 --> 00:07:54.640
Well, the good part
about being grandparents,

00:07:54.640 --> 00:07:56.160
you get to give them back
after you play with them.

00:07:56.160 --> 00:07:58.070
- That's right, that's right.

00:07:58.070 --> 00:07:59.270
- Yeah, so, well, speaking of family,

00:07:59.270 --> 00:08:00.920
your husband just released a new book

00:08:00.920 --> 00:08:03.540
featuring immigrant paintings and stories.

00:08:03.540 --> 00:08:06.250
When he started painting, he
wasn't as good as he is now,

00:08:06.250 --> 00:08:07.083
I've heard.

00:08:07.083 --> 00:08:08.990
So how did you, as a
teacher, encourage his work,

00:08:08.990 --> 00:08:11.963
or was this like the speeches
and you just laid low?

00:08:12.990 --> 00:08:13.853
- I laid low.

00:08:16.380 --> 00:08:19.820
But actually, he's really gotten good.

00:08:19.820 --> 00:08:20.653
I'm shocked.

00:08:20.653 --> 00:08:23.610
I mean, he was not
interested in art at all.

00:08:23.610 --> 00:08:26.360
The White House has a major
collection of American art,

00:08:26.360 --> 00:08:28.653
and, of course, he never
really looked at it.

00:08:29.700 --> 00:08:32.250
So I was very surprised
when he took up painting,

00:08:32.250 --> 00:08:35.230
but he's gotten to be really good.

00:08:35.230 --> 00:08:36.850
That was one of his first paintings.

00:08:36.850 --> 00:08:38.130
- [Kelly] That's amazing.

00:08:38.130 --> 00:08:40.170
- But now, of course,
these are the portraits

00:08:40.170 --> 00:08:43.520
of immigrants that are at
the Bush Center in Dallas.

00:08:43.520 --> 00:08:45.030
And I want to invite everybody,

00:08:45.030 --> 00:08:48.910
come to the Bush Center
and see the paintings.

00:08:48.910 --> 00:08:52.160
They're immigrants that you
know, like Henry Kissinger

00:08:52.160 --> 00:08:55.750
or Madeleine Albright, or then
people that you don't know

00:08:55.750 --> 00:08:57.940
that have immigrated to the United States,

00:08:57.940 --> 00:09:01.450
and it's their paintings, their
portraits and their stories.

00:09:01.450 --> 00:09:02.809
- Oh man, they're beautiful.

00:09:02.809 --> 00:09:04.750
I have tried so hard to paint.

00:09:04.750 --> 00:09:08.460
I tell you what, I am the worst at it.

00:09:08.460 --> 00:09:11.060
So, well, we've found this
quote of yours, and I love it.

00:09:11.060 --> 00:09:12.590
You said, "When you're married

00:09:12.590 --> 00:09:14.350
to the President of the United States,

00:09:14.350 --> 00:09:17.580
you don't worry too much about
him leaving his wet towels

00:09:17.580 --> 00:09:20.700
on the floor, but in Dallas,
things are different.

00:09:20.700 --> 00:09:22.650
Memo to the ex presidents,

00:09:22.650 --> 00:09:26.165
turmoil in East Timor
is no longer an excuse

00:09:26.165 --> 00:09:28.297
not to pick up your socks."

00:09:29.326 --> 00:09:30.576
That's amazing.

00:09:31.840 --> 00:09:34.870
Note to self. You can do it now.

00:09:34.870 --> 00:09:38.123
So has your husband gotten used
to picking up his own socks?

00:09:39.070 --> 00:09:40.313
- No, not really.

00:09:42.127 --> 00:09:44.370
- I love you. That's awesome.

00:09:44.370 --> 00:09:47.190
Okay, well, we had Jenna on this show.

00:09:47.190 --> 00:09:48.550
I love your daughter by the way.

00:09:48.550 --> 00:09:49.383
She's so fun.

00:09:49.383 --> 00:09:50.900
I kind of feel like we
were separated at birth

00:09:50.900 --> 00:09:52.400
every time we hang out.

00:09:52.400 --> 00:09:55.550
But she mentioned that the
White House was haunted.

00:09:55.550 --> 00:09:57.340
Did you ever experience that?

00:09:57.340 --> 00:09:58.500
- No, we'd never,

00:09:58.500 --> 00:10:00.080
I think Barbara and Jenna were the ones

00:10:00.080 --> 00:10:02.010
that felt like there
was, that it was haunted,

00:10:02.010 --> 00:10:04.730
and pretended like they heard voices,

00:10:04.730 --> 00:10:06.820
and it's probably just the Secret Service

00:10:06.820 --> 00:10:09.350
talking down the hall in
another room that they heard.

00:10:09.350 --> 00:10:11.710
But we didn't think it was haunted.

00:10:11.710 --> 00:10:12.543
We loved living there.

00:10:12.543 --> 00:10:14.600
It's a magnificent house,

00:10:14.600 --> 00:10:17.164
and it has a magnificent art collection,

00:10:17.164 --> 00:10:20.900
which was also fun to have
the chance to live with

00:10:20.900 --> 00:10:22.850
those beautiful paintings.

00:10:22.850 --> 00:10:25.570
- It's definitely a beautiful house.

00:10:25.570 --> 00:10:27.470
I will say, though, I've
been there a few times,

00:10:27.470 --> 00:10:29.390
and living there would
have been a little daunting

00:10:29.390 --> 00:10:30.882
for a kid, I think.

00:10:30.882 --> 00:10:32.190
I think I would have been terrified.

00:10:32.190 --> 00:10:33.894
Okay. So I'm not sure if you know this,

00:10:33.894 --> 00:10:37.520
but today is national no dirty dishes day.

00:10:37.520 --> 00:10:39.620
So who washes more dishes in your house?

00:10:39.620 --> 00:10:40.860
I think I know this answer.

00:10:40.860 --> 00:10:41.890
You or President Bush?

00:10:41.890 --> 00:10:42.840
- Well, you know the answer.

